WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.533
Reward.

2
00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:03.416
One number defines the goal.

3
00:00:03.416 --> 00:00:06.300
Get it wrong, and the agent will find the loophole.

4
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.667
The reward is the scalar signal that defines the goal.

5
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:13.751
It is the only thing the agent optimizes.

6
00:00:13.751 --> 00:00:16.400
Not your intent, not the documentation.

7
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.800
That is why reward design is where RL projects live or die.

8
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.633
There are two styles of reward.

9
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:26.223
Sparse rewards pay only at the goal.

10
00:00:26.223 --> 00:00:30.167
Honest, but slow, because the agent may never find the signal.

11
00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:34.367
Shaping adds small costs or partial credit, which speeds learning.

12
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.633
But every shaping term is a new loophole to audit.

13
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:41.200
Watch how one line transforms behavior.

14
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.467
With a step cost of zero, any path pays plus one, so the agent wanders forever.

15
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:52.833
With minus zero point zero one, every wasted step hurts, and the policy becomes a beeline.

16
00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:58.133
With minus zero point two, detours cost more than a crater, so it dives in.

17
00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:00.200
A reward bug, not an agent bug.

18
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.667
Here is the entire spec in four lines.

19
00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:07.767
Reaching the goal pays plus one, and the crater costs minus one.

20
00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.276
Every other step pays the step cost.

21
00:01:10.276 --> 00:01:13.100
That single knob is what changed the behavior.

22
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:18.967
You reward a survey probe plus point one whenever its solar panels are charging.

23
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:21.767
You hope it stays powered while mapping terrain.

24
00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:26.021
Instead it parks facing the sun and spins gently forever.

25
00:01:26.021 --> 00:01:28.033
Maximum reward, zero mapping.

26
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:33.967
That is reward hacking, the same disease as an LLM learning to flatter its reward model.

27
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:39.833
The common mistake is rewarding a convenient proxy and hoping for the best.

28
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:46.500
Reward the real outcome instead, keep shaping small, and always ask what loophole the agent could find.

29
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.535
Let's recap.

30
00:01:48.535 --> 00:01:51.667
Reward is the only thing the agent optimizes.

31
00:01:51.967 --> 00:01:55.800
Sparse rewards are honest but slow, and shaping adds loopholes.

32
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.733
One reward line can change all behavior.

33
00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:01.616
So always ask what loophole it found.

34
00:02:01.616 --> 00:02:02.733
Next up: policy.
